## 2. UX Principles

### Prefer

- searchable dropdowns for selecting existing records;
- autocomplete for large or remote datasets;
- context-aware suggestions ranked by useful operational factors;
- live validation before submission;
- inline, actionable guidance beside the relevant field;
- sensible and visible defaults;
- disabled impossible actions with an explanation;
- structured date, time, status, category, and relationship inputs;
- automatic population from authenticated-user, organisation, and selected-record context;
- clear loading, empty, success, partial, stale, and error states;
- permission-aware navigation and controls;
- confirmation only when an action is materially destructive or difficult to reverse;
- undo, restore, archive, or grace-period patterns where appropriate;
- keyboard-operable and screen-reader-compatible controls;
- layouts that work at 320 CSS pixels without loss of function.

### Avoid

- free-text identifiers, names, foreign keys, or status values;
- asking users to type IDs that the system already knows;
- duplicate entry of data held elsewhere;
- native browser alerts and confirmations;
- unnecessary confirmation dialogs for safe, reversible actions;
- validation shown only after submission;
- controls that allow an invalid value and reject it later when prevention is possible;
- workflows that require training to discover the next action;
- hidden permissions or unexplained disabled controls;
- fabricated fallback values after an API failure;
- success notifications for browser-only state when persistence is expected;
- colour as the only way to communicate status.

### Interaction requirements

- Forms MUST show field-specific validation and a clear submission summary.
- Focus MUST move predictably when dialogs open, errors occur, and navigation changes.
- Searchable controls MUST support keyboard selection, a visible focus state, accessible labels, and a no-results state.
- Destructive controls MUST state the affected record and consequence.
- Lists MUST provide filtering or search before they become operationally difficult to scan.
- The interface MUST preserve user input after a recoverable server or network error.
- A protected page MUST NOT render protected content before identity and permissions resolve.

---

## 3. Backend Principles

All security and business validation MUST exist server-side. Frontend validation improves experience only and MUST NOT be treated as an enforcement boundary.

The backend MUST enforce:

- authentication and permission checks;
- organisation/tenant isolation on every applicable read and write;
- ownership of every referenced record;
- concurrency protection for conflicting operations;
- business invariants and allowed state transitions;
- duplicate prevention where duplicates would create operational ambiguity;
- active, archived, deleted, and eligibility state;
- audit logging for security-sensitive and operationally important actions;
- historical preservation;
- soft deletion or archival where restoration or auditability is required;
- input size, shape, type, and allowed-value constraints;
- transaction atomicity between a business change and its required audit event.

### Backend implementation rules

- Authorisation SHOULD use one reusable permission mechanism; endpoint-specific compatibility logic MUST be temporary, documented, and tested.
- Tenant scope SHOULD be applied in repository/service/query helpers so it cannot be accidentally omitted.
- Mutations spanning related records MUST use a transaction.
- Conflict-prone mutations MUST use database constraints, locking, isolation, or another reliable concurrency strategy.
- Time MUST have an explicit timezone policy. Stored instants SHOULD be UTC and timezone-aware; organisation-local dates MUST use the organisation timezone.
- Sensitive values MUST NOT appear in logs, errors, audit details, URLs, or normal API responses.
- External or slow side effects SHOULD run through durable jobs with retry and idempotency.
- Multi-instance features MUST NOT rely on process-local memory for shared state.
- Business logic SHOULD live in focused services, not route handlers or UI components.

---

## 4. Database Principles

- Schemas MUST be normalised unless a measured performance requirement justifies denormalisation.
- Canonical facts MUST have one source of truth.
- Relationships MUST use foreign keys rather than copied names or manually entered IDs.
- Duplicate facts MUST NOT be stored in multiple domain models without explicit synchronisation ownership.
- Historical records MUST remain readable after a related user, team, site, or organisation record is archived.
- Deletion policy MUST be explicit per entity: hard delete, soft delete, archive, retention, and restore.
- Database constraints MUST enforce invariants that cannot safely depend on application code alone.
- Uniqueness MUST reflect tenant boundaries and normalisation rules, including case sensitivity.
- Indexes MUST support tenant filters, common searches, joins, time ranges, and availability/conflict checks.
- Date/time columns representing instants SHOULD be timezone-aware.
- Schema changes MUST use Alembic in every persistent environment.
- A migration MUST upgrade a fresh supported PostgreSQL database, upgrade an existing supported database, and have an intentional rollback strategy.
- Application startup MUST NOT silently create or alter production schema.
- Large migrations MUST account for locks, backfills, deployment ordering, and backward compatibility.
- Schema design SHOULD allow likely enterprise expansion without embedding hypothetical fields or duplicating current data.

---

## 5. API Standards

### Resource and route conventions

- New APIs MUST use the canonical versioned `/api/v1` surface.
- Resource names MUST be plural nouns; actions SHOULD be represented by state transitions or explicit subresources.
- A concept MUST NOT have competing canonical endpoints or models.
- Request and response schemas MUST be explicit and separate from ORM persistence models.
- Create operations SHOULD return `201`; successful no-content deletion SHOULD return `204`; other status codes MUST be consistent by operation type.

### Errors

Errors MUST use one structured contract containing, at minimum:

- a stable machine-readable error code;
- a safe human-readable message;
- field-level details when applicable;
- a correlation/request identifier;
- no secrets or internal stack details.

Validation, permission, conflict, not-found, and rate-limit errors MUST be distinguishable without parsing prose.

### Collections

- Collection endpoints MUST use consistent pagination with metadata.
- Filtering, sorting, and search parameter names MUST be consistent.
- Page sizes MUST be bounded.
- Filters MUST preserve tenant scope and permission rules.

### Trust and compatibility

- No endpoint may trust tenant IDs, permission claims, computed totals, eligibility, or ownership supplied by frontend state.
- Mutation endpoints SHOULD support idempotency when retries could create duplicates.
- Breaking API changes require a migration/deprecation plan.
- API documentation and tests MUST reflect the active contract.

---

## 6. Frontend Standards

- Components MUST be small enough to understand and test independently.
- The application shell MUST NOT become a monolithic feature container.
- Domain screens, hooks, services, state, and reusable UI controls SHOULD live in focused modules.
- All API traffic MUST pass through a central client responsible for base URL, authentication, safe error parsing, cancellation, and token-expiry behaviour.
- Shared controls MUST be reused for searchable selectors, tables, dialogs, validation messages, permission guards, loading states, empty states, and error states.
- Server data SHOULD use a consistent query/cache strategy with explicit invalidation after mutations.
- Protected routes MUST wait for authentication and permission resolution.
- Permission-aware UI is a usability feature, not an authorisation boundary.
- Direct URL navigation, refresh, back/forward navigation, and deep linking MUST work.
- Locally generated records MUST NOT impersonate persisted backend records.
- Optimistic updates MAY be used only when rollback and conflict behaviour are defined.
- Runtime configuration MUST fail clearly when required production values are absent; it MUST NOT silently fall back to localhost in production.
- Repeated form, table, and state-transition logic SHOULD be schema- or component-driven.
- Mobile and keyboard behaviour MUST be covered by representative tests.

### Incremental application decomposition

`App.js` decomposition MUST happen incrementally during Operations Core
development. Every workflow that is materially changed SHOULD be moved into its
intended feature module where practical. The team MUST NOT wait for a standalone
monolith rewrite, and `App.js` MUST NOT be expanded further without documented
justification.

---

## 7. Human Error Prevention Checklist

Every feature design and review MUST answer:

- Can invalid data be entered?
- Can duplicate data be entered?
- Can an impossible action be attempted?
- Can a concurrency conflict create an invalid outcome?
- Can conflicts be detected earlier?
- Can typing be reduced without removing necessary flexibility?
- Can known data be selected instead of re-entered?
- Can defaults help, and are they visible and safe?
- Can recommendations help, and is their reasoning understandable?
- Can a user accidentally act on another organisation's data?
- Can users accidentally lose data?
- Can destructive actions be reversed?
- Can a retry or double-click duplicate the operation?
- Can stale information cause the wrong decision?
- Can an inaccessible control prevent task completion?
- Can a user mistake a placeholder, cached value, or frontend-only value for persisted truth?
- Is the error message placed where the user can correct the problem?
- Does the audit trail explain who changed what and when?

A feature is incomplete when a material risk is identified but neither prevented nor explicitly accepted.

---

## 8. Feature Design Process

Before implementing a significant feature, the engineer MUST briefly document:

1. **Business problem:** user, operational decision, desired outcome, and cost of error.
2. **Two or three viable approaches:** including meaningful trade-offs.
3. **Recommended approach:** architecture and user flow.
4. **Why it scales:** data ownership, query shape, concurrency, extensibility, and operational support.
5. **How it reduces mistakes:** prevention, validation, defaults, recommendations, reversibility, and visibility.
6. **Data and permission boundaries:** authoritative records, tenant scope, roles/permissions, and sensitive fields.
7. **Success measures:** acceptance criteria, tests, performance expectations, and observability.

Implementation then follows this sequence:

1. Confirm the canonical domain model and API.
2. Define invariants, permissions, tenant scope, lifecycle, and audit events.
3. Design the shortest safe user flow, including loading, empty, error, mobile, and accessibility states.
4. Identify concurrency and idempotency risks.
5. Define migration and backward-compatibility needs.
6. Implement backend authority and database constraints.
7. Implement the frontend using shared primitives.
8. Test positive, negative, cross-tenant, permission, concurrency, accessibility, and failure paths.
9. Run migration, schema-drift, build, secret, and diff checks.
10. Review the completed workflow against this document.

Exceptions MUST name the violated standard, reason, risk, owner, compensating control, and removal date.

### Next product milestone

> A complete Operations Workspace from which a manager can understand staffing,
> schedule patrols, resolve conflicts, monitor deployments, and respond to
> current incidents without jumping between disconnected pages.

Work contributing to this milestone MUST be reviewed as a coherent daily
operations workflow, not as a collection of independent modules.

---

## 9. Enterprise Readiness Checklist

Before a feature is complete, verify:

- [ ] permissions are least-privilege and tested by role;
- [ ] tenant isolation is enforced and cross-tenant tests exist;
- [ ] mobile layout and touch targets support the complete workflow;
- [ ] keyboard and screen-reader behaviour is usable;
- [ ] important actions and denials have suitable audit coverage;
- [ ] the API follows route, schema, error, pagination, and status conventions;
- [ ] relationships, constraints, indexes, deletion, history, and migrations preserve database integrity;
- [ ] unit, integration, permission, tenant, failure, and regression tests pass;
- [ ] queries avoid unbounded reads and known N+1 patterns;
- [ ] concurrent and retried mutations remain correct;
- [ ] logs, metrics, correlation IDs, and alerts make failures diagnosable;
- [ ] the design can support expected enterprise volume and likely extensions;
- [ ] no placeholder behaviour is presented as complete;
- [ ] no secrets, credentials, personal data, or care-sensitive data are exposed;
- [ ] documentation and operational runbooks reflect the final behaviour.

