| H-01 | Schema authority | Development startup calls `Base.metadata.create_all()` and maintains a manual SQLite `ALTER TABLE` compatibility map alongside Alembic. This permits schema paths to diverge. | Make Alembic the only persistent-schema authority. Use migrated disposable databases for development and remove manual schema mutation after a documented transition. | M |
| H-02 | Frontend architecture | `frontend/src/App.js` is approximately 3,372 lines and contains navigation, authentication, API access, domain screens, dialogs, forms, and business calculations. | Split by domain route with a small shell, central API client, shared query/state primitives, reusable form controls, and independently testable components. | XL |
| H-03 | Routing | Page selection is primarily local `activeNav` state rather than canonical URL routing. Deep links, refresh, browser navigation, support links, and route-level permission handling are limited. | Add accessible URL routing, guarded routes, not-found/forbidden screens, preserved intended destinations, and navigation tests. | M |
| H-04 | Distributed security | Rate limiting is stored in a process-local Python dictionary keyed by IP and path. It resets on restart, differs per instance, and can grow without a global bound. | Use a shared, bounded rate limiter or trusted edge enforcement, define proxy-aware identity rules, and add endpoint-specific policies and observability. | M |
| H-05 | Transactional audit | Several CRUD paths commit the business record before writing the audit event; notifications and related writes may use additional commits. A failure can leave incomplete history. | Define a unit-of-work boundary so required business state and audit outbox/event are committed atomically; deliver side effects after commit. | L |
